---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import PageToc from "../../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../../styles/extended.css";
import metadata from "../../../data/standards/hgb/drs22.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  subtitle={metadata.nativeTitle}
  framework="hgb"
  status={metadata.status}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Scope" },        { id: "s2", label: "Eigenkapitalspiegel" },        { id: "s3", label: "NCI" },        { id: "s4", label: "Worked example" },        { id: "s5", label: "IFRS" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Scope – Part of a Coordinated 2016 Release</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid">
<div class="card info span2">
<div class="ctitle">One of three standards announced together</div>
<div class="principle">DRS 22, announced in the same 23 February 2016 Bundesanzeiger notice as DRS 23 and DRS 24, elaborates the presentation of consolidated group equity – supplementing §297(1) HGB's own Eigenkapitalspiegel (statement of changes in equity) requirement, established in the earlier HGB poster series, and directly building on the NCI (non-controlling interest) mechanics established under §307 HGB in the Vollkonsolidierung poster.</div>
</div>
</div><div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>The Eigenkapitalspiegel – Movement, Not Just a Snapshot</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="cols-1">
<div class="card">
<div class="ctitle">What the statement actually shows</div>
<div class="principle">DRS 22 requires the statement to show the FULL MOVEMENT of each group equity component across the period – opening balance, net income attributable, other comprehensive-income-type items (currency translation differences, per DRS 25), dividends, capital transactions – not merely opening and closing balances alone, giving readers a genuine bridge between the two balance sheet dates.</div>
</div>
<div class="card">
<div class="ctitle">Currency translation differences, specifically</div>
<div class="principle">DRS 22 requires the accumulated currency translation adjustment arising from consolidating foreign subsidiaries (per DRS 25's own modified closing rate method) to be shown as its OWN distinct equity component – genuinely useful given how this item can swing significantly year to year purely from exchange rate movements, unrelated to the group's own operating performance.</div>
</div>
</div><div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Non-Controlling Interests, Elaborated</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid">
<div class="card warn span2">
<div class="ctitle">Building on §307's own "losses even into deficit" rule</div>
<div class="principle">Recall from the earlier HGB Vollkonsolidierung poster: §307 requires NCI losses to be attributed even into a deficit position, converging with IFRS 10. DRS 22 elaborates the PRESENTATION mechanics for this – showing NCI as its own separate equity component, with its own movement bridge, and specific guidance on presenting a genuinely negative NCI balance where cumulative losses have exceeded the non-controlling shareholders' own original contribution.</div>
</div>
</div><div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Worked Example – NCI Moving Into a Deficit Position</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid">
<div class="card span2">
<div class="worked">
<div class="wk-title">Presenting a negative non-controlling interest balance</div>
<div class="wk-body">A subsidiary, 70% owned by the parent and 30% owned by outside investors, has an opening NCI balance of €200,000. The subsidiary incurs a €900,000 loss for the year, with the NCI's own 30% share being €270,000.</div>
<div class="wk-answer">Under §307 (attributing losses even into deficit) and DRS 22's own presentation guidance, the NCI balance moves from €200,000 to €200,000 − €270,000 = <strong>−€70,000</strong> – presented as its OWN distinct, clearly-labelled negative equity component in the Eigenkapitalspiegel, rather than being floored at zero or absorbed elsewhere in the group's own equity.</div>
</div>
</div>
</div><div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Relationship to IFRS (IAS 1)</h2><span class="line"></span></div><div class="grid">
<div class="card span2">
<div class="ctitle">Broadly converged presentation, with the same underlying goodwill-driven divergence</div>
<div class="principle">DRS 22's statement of changes in equity closely parallels IAS 1's own requirement. The main lingering divergence isn't in DRS 22 itself, but flows through from earlier in this series: HGB's own mandatory goodwill amortisation (versus IFRS's impairment-only approach) means the RETAINED EARNINGS component this statement tracks will genuinely differ in trajectory between the two frameworks, even where the presentation FORMAT itself is very similar.</div>
</div>
</div>
</StandardPageLayout>
